    'set_channel_budget': dict({
      'description': '''
        Set or replace a channel's spending budget. Admin-only.
        <blank line>
        Once the channel's spend in the window reaches ``limit_usd`` (a
        decimal string such as ``"25.00"``; ``"0"`` stops the channel), new
        turns there are refused, including routines that post there. Spend
        is the tenant's debits for the channel, markup included.
        <blank line>
        ``window``: ``monthly`` resets each UTC calendar month and takes no
        dates; ``total`` counts everything since ``starts_at`` (or ever);
        ``fixed`` counts only between ``starts_at`` and ``ends_at`` and gates
        nothing outside them. Dates are ISO 8601, read as UTC without an
        offset. A thread id resolves to its parent channel.
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'channel_id': dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'ends_at': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'limit_usd': dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'starts_at': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'window': dict({
            'enum': list([
              'monthly',
              'total',
              'fixed',
            ]),
            'type': 'string',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'channel_id',
          'limit_usd',
          'window',
        ]),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_channel_environment': dict({
      'description': '''
        Choose the environment a channel's turns run in, or the workspace default.
        For example, run #growth in the pymc environment. Changes where the agent runs,
        not which agent answers; use ``set_agent_default`` for that.
        <blank line>
        Omit ``channel_id`` to set the workspace default, which every channel without
        its own environment uses. The environment must already exist
        (``list_environments``). Conversations pick it up from their next message.
        Setting either default requires an admin. Unrestricted networking in
        a sealed channel needs an admin and their confirmation, as does a
        workspace default sealed channels would run in: pass
        ``confirm_open_network=true`` only after the caller confirms it.
        <blank line>
        Pass the parent channel's id: ``parent_channel_id`` from turn_controls; a
        thread id resolves to its parent. The caller must be able to see the channel.
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'channel_id': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'confirm_open_network': dict({
            'default': False,
            'type': 'boolean',
          }),
          'environment_name': dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'environment_name',
        ]),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_channel_rule': dict({
      'description': '''
        Set who can read a channel and who can post there. Requires an
        admin. Leave one out to keep it.
        <blank line>
        ``readers``: ``any``; ``inside``, only turns in this channel read its messages
        and conversations; or ``own``, which also keeps it to its own agents: only they
        run and show there, and they run and show nowhere else. ``writers``: ``any``;
        ``own`` (only with readers ``own``); or ``none``, nothing posts there, daimon
        included.
        <blank line>
        To isolate a channel so nothing leaks in or out, set readers and writers
        ``own``. Its default agent becomes its own agent, by an agent rule naming the
        channel alone; it must be custom and answer nowhere else. Otherwise pass
        ``copy_from`` (an agent name, usually the one answering there) to copy that
        agent, without credentials, as the channel's default. To make agent X the only
        agent in a channel, set the channel's default to X first. ``release_agents``
        drops the rules of the agents kept to the channel, once readers aren't ``own``.
        Rules are per channel: ``channel_id`` names a channel, never a thread. Pass
        the parent channel's id from turn_controls (``parent_channel_id``).
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'channel_id': dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'copy_from': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'readers': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'enum': list([
                  'any',
                  'inside',
                  'own',
                ]),
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'release_agents': dict({
            'default': False,
            'type': 'boolean',
          }),
          'writers': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'enum': list([
                  'any',
                  'own',
                  'none',
                ]),
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'channel_id',
        ]),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_display_identity': dict({
      'description': '''
        Change how Daimon appears in this Discord server: its display name,
        its avatar, or both.
        <blank line>
        Use when the user asks you to rename yourself or change your profile
        picture. ``display_name`` is the new nickname (1-32 characters).
        ``avatar_url`` is the URL of an image the user attached to a message
        (a cdn.discordapp.com or media.discordapp.net link); png, jpeg, gif
        and webp work. Both apply to the whole server: Discord has no
        per-channel identity, so tell the user when they asked for one
        channel. There is no reset yet: an empty ``display_name`` is treated
        as omitted, so a name cannot be cleared back to the default. Needs a
        server admin. Pass this turn's origin_context_id: a pinned agent, or a
        turn in a confidential channel, can't change it. Discord-only.
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'avatar_url': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'display_name': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'origin_context_id': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
        }),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_plain_text_mentions': dict({
      'description': '''
        Let a typed "@<your name>" in a channel or thread start a reply. Admin-only.
        <blank line>
        For the whole workspace. When
        ``enabled`` is true, a person's message in a channel or thread that
        contains your name after "@" as plain text (any case, for example a
        pasted prompt or a mention typed on a phone without picking you from
        the list) is answered as if they had mentioned you. Messages from
        bots and webhooks never count, and direct messages are unchanged. So
        that suffixes and domains never match, a name followed by "(" or by a
        dot and a letter is ignored ("@Daimon (urgent)", "@Daimon.Thanks"). Off
        by default. Read the returned ``note`` back to the user.
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'enabled': dict({
            'type': 'boolean',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'enabled',
        ]),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_progress_plans': dict({
      'description': '''
        Turn progress plans on or off where you are asked to.
        <blank line>
        A progress plan is a short checklist of a longer task's steps on the
        progress card. Call this when someone asks, for example "turn on
        progress plans here".
        <blank line>
        Scope: ``thread_id`` and ``channel_id`` (this turn's thread and channel
        ids) for a thread; ``channel_id`` alone for a channel; neither for the
        workspace. Channel and workspace scope need an admin.
        <blank line>
        Modes: ``on``, ``off``, ``disabled`` (lock a channel or workspace so
        nothing below can turn it on; not valid for a thread), ``inherit``
        (drop this scope's setting). Read the returned ``note`` back to the user.
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'channel_id': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
          'mode': dict({
            'enum': list([
              'on',
              'off',
              'disabled',
              'inherit',
            ]),
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'thread_id': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'mode',
        ]),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_routine_sandbox_tools': dict({
      'description': '''
        Turn built-in sandbox tools (bash, file reads and edits and the rest) on or off
        for one routine's own scheduled runs. Admin only.
        <blank line>
        They are off for every unattended run by default. The supported way today is the
        operator's ``daimon routines sandbox-tools``. This tool accepts only a live platform
        admin's own token, re-checked against the server on each call; no Daimon
        surface issues such a token yet, and the tool is kept for a future admin surface.
        Owning the routine is not enough, and agent keys, agents in a conversation, operator
        tokens, legacy tokens and other platforms are refused. DM routines cannot be opted
        in. An opt-in covers exactly the routine's current agent version and environment:
        once either changes, runs go without built-ins and the opt-in is cleared. Each
        change records who made it and when, and ``get_routine``/``list_routines`` show it
        as ``sandbox_tools_enabled``. It takes effect from the routine's next run. Setting
        the value a routine already has changes nothing. Timers, watches, agent chat, fleet
        workers, ``daimon run`` and checkpoints cannot be opted in.
      ''',
      'parameters': dict({
        'additionalProperties': False,
        'properties': dict({
          'enabled': dict({
            'type': 'boolean',
          }),
          'routine_id': dict({
            'format': 'uuid',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'routine_id',
          'enabled',
        ]),
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'set_setup_target': dict({
      'description': '''
        Switch this setup conversation to an explicitly selected MA agent identity.
        <blank line>
        Pass the current turn's origin_context_id and a current agent's MA id.
        State the target switch briefly. Other running turns keep their own target.
